            kunci:
              'a) LoRa (Long Range) is a low-power wireless communication protocol with a long transmission distance between connected devices. LoRa operates in the sub-GHz frequency spectrum and is known for its ability to transmit data over a wider range while consuming very little power. LoRa is widely used in IoT systems because it is ideal for applications that require low data rates and long battery life, such as smart agriculture, smart cities, industrial monitoring, and environmental sensing.\n\nb) Here are the differences:\n- LoRa is a modulation technology (a way of modulating radio signals) used for long-distance, low-power communication. Its focus is on the physical layer, similar to a language for "how to send signals." LoRa does not define a network protocol, only how data is carried over the air.\n- LoRaWAN is a network protocol that runs on top of LoRa. It defines how LoRa devices communicate at scale: message formats, security, authentication, device management, and communication through gateways to servers.\n\nc) Advantages of LoRa compared to Wi-Fi and Bluetooth in IoT applications:\n- Very long range, which can exceed 5-15 km.\n- Very low power consumption, suitable for battery-powered devices, lasting for months.\n- Resistant to interference; its modulation (chirp spread spectrum) makes it more stable in areas with signal interference.\n- Can be used in remote areas with inadequate internet network infrastructure.\n\nd) Characteristics of applications suitable for LoRaWAN:\n- Requires a communication range of up to several kilometers;\n- Uses battery-powered devices that must last a long time;\n- Has many sensors spread over a wide area;\n- Only transmits small data sizes at specific intervals;\n- Does not require high data rates or real-time communication;\n- Requires security, device authentication, and centralized network management.',
