            kunci:
              "The following are the LoRaWAN class types:\n- Class A\nClass A LoRa devices are known for their high power efficiency. After each transmission (uplink), these devices open only two short receive windows and then sleep again, making them ideal for battery-powered sensors and devices focused primarily on data transmission. While Class A devices can effectively conserve power, they may not receive data as frequently as other classes. However, they provide a balance between power savings and occasional data reception, making them suitable for a variety of IoT applications.\n- Class B\nClass B LoRa devices are designed to provide scheduled reception windows synchronized with network time, making them suitable for applications with specific and time-sensitive communication requirements. These devices offer more predictable communication opportunities than Class A devices, as they combine power-saving features while allowing for scheduled and intermittent data reception. This allows them to efficiently serve applications that require timely data delivery while still conserving power for extended battery life.\n- Class C\nLoRa Class C devices are known for their near-continuous reception capabilities with only brief interruptions during transmission. These devices are suitable for applications that require near-real-time, two-way communication, as they can receive data at almost any time. However, these devices are less power-efficient than Class A and Class B devices, making them more suitable for applications with continuous power sources or higher power budgets that prioritize consistent communication.",
